From 858935670cd3e8d1c7a79fedd9a870e679bfd7e2 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Daniel Boyd <danieljboyd@icloud.com>
Date: Tue, 6 Oct 2026 11:23:43 -0500
Subject: [PATCH 3/5] x11: an alpha channel for borderless windows, when a
 theme asks

A theme that draws menus and tool tips with rounded corners, as GNOME's
are, needs what it leaves transparent at the corners to show what is
behind the window.  The backend creates every window with the screen's
default visual, which has no alpha channel, so the corners show black or
the window's background.

With the new GSBackBorderlessWindowAlpha default set to YES (a theme can
set it in the GSThemeDomain of its Info.plist), a borderless window is
created with a 32-bit visual with an alpha channel, starting transparent,
when a compositing manager runs (it owns the _NET_WM_CM_S<n> selection)
and the screen has such a visual.  Without a compositing manager
transparent pixels would show black, so windows keep the default visual
then.  Windows with a style are left alone.  Nothing changes unless the
default is set.

Testing: Tests/x11/borderlessalpha.m forks a stand-in compositing
manager that only owns _NET_WM_CM_S0 (Tests/x11/FakeCompositor.h, used
again by the shadow tests that follow), then creates windows and reads
back the depth of their X windows.  It checks the default off, the
default on with a compositing manager, a styled window with the default
on, and the default on once the compositing manager has gone.  The depth
of the X window is what decides whether transparency can show at all;
the off and the no-compositor cases guard against windows getting an
alpha channel where it would show black.  It doesn't look at the pixels
the gui draws.  Without this change the "has an alpha channel" check
fails; with it all four pass.  The test needs a display with no
compositing manager (a private Xvfb) and skips otherwise.

AI assistance: this change, its test and this description were written
with Claude (Anthropic's Claude Code).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
